    "episode_id": 84969,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/4/84969.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "like they were planning to actually take children away from parents in a strategy of inflicting trauma to deter immigration. And I thought, no, there's no way any American government would ever employ the strategy of harming children as a political strategy. It just would never happen here, not a Republican administration, not a Democratic administration. And yet that's what it sounded like. And a member of my team, as I was saying, there's no way they're going to actually do this. A member of my team said, well, there's one way to find out. Go down to the border. And I thought, well, I guess I should go because if it's happening, we need to see it and we need to call it out. And so that's Sunday. I flew down and I went first to the Customs and Border Protection Processing Center. It goes by the name Ursula Street. It's there in McAllen, Texas. So I go in, I see the ice cold holding cells that are often talked about. Then I saw a room full of monitors that they do remote interviews. And then there's a warehouse. And as I go into the warehouse, I see these large 30 foot square chain link cages. and they want to show me their first day. I don't want to see your first aid station. I don't want to see your kitchen. What's going on with these cages? And that's where they were separating boys into one, girls into another, fathers, mothers. We stopped in front of this one cage and the boys were lining up by height to get ready to get a meal. And the shortest one was in front, and he was just knee-high to a grasshopper, maybe four years old. And I said, you took these kids away from their parents, and they said, well, not all of them. And I said, well, by that you mean there were some older kids who crossed the border by themselves. They said, yes, but the rest. And they said, yes, we took them away. It's where do you do that? They said, well, when we come out of the interview room, that's where we separate them. And there were actually separations going on at other places in the system. But I thought of it. I was stunned. I was stunned. When I went outside and described it to the press, the press was stunned. This was happening. And the Washington Post, when I described it, gave me Pinocchio's because they said, I think they just couldn't believe that what I was saying was true. And as it turned out, it was true. And everyone calls them cages because that's what they look like. That wasn't the end of the day. Because then I heard from immigration advocates that about an hour away in Brownsville that the separated boys were being stuffed into a former Walmart by the hundreds. And so we drove up there, Ray, Zeke, I remember my team and myself. And they had already told us in advance they wouldn't let us in. But I thought, I just knocked on their door. Why not? What are they going to say? If they won't let me in, I'll invite the supervisor to come out and talk to me. Well, they were not happy. I was there. And the assistant supervisor said that the supervisor would come out. He didn't come out. I called back 10 minutes later. Actually, what was going on is he called the police. And so he came out after the police car started arriving. And I thought for a moment, I thought, well, I've never actually been handcuffed and slammed to the ground. It might be the first time about right now. Do you have like a special, I mean, U.S. Senator ID? Is there, you know, I mean, I can't imagine an average police officer would necessarily, you know, know who you are or what did they say when you said I was a U.S. Senator and I, you know, kind of demand to see what's going on here. I did not hesitate to mention that I was a U.S. Senator. But they didn't ask me for ID, they did ask me.",
