    "turn_text": "doesn't like this decision because they want to lock up children in prison. So if we go back to, I want to go back to Chaltever and just put this in context. Plan A was to traumatize children through child separation. When a judge in the end of June, last year, said you can't do that, and you must reunify the parents and children, they went to Plan B, which was to traumatize children through incarceration. We do have some family internment camps, currently, three of them, but they're limited to the three days in theory, or 20 days during a period of high influx, and that period is not well identified, so in general, 20 days. So the administration says, well, let's just lock them up for a long time. So a group of us held parallel hearings in the US Senate, a group of senators, to bring in child experts to answer the question you're asking. And imprisonment induces an uncertainty about life. You're being cut off from almost all your connections. You have no idea if you'll ever make a living. You're treated as a prisoner. which is a decisions are made for you. You will eat this, you will move here, you will do that. And it is deeply damaging to individuals. When I hear the experts talk about how the brain gets rewired with the stress of incarceration and how that trauma lasts through extensive period, I'm just like, this is brutal. This is brutal. I went to a dilly where women and their children were incarcerated and because there was a family that was trapped in limbo that had been there five months, so they hadn't been released after 20 days. And the mother, I was alerted to this by an immigration advocate, so I met with the mother and she just sobbed for about 45 minutes of conversation. Her daughter, 14 years old, wasn't eating, wasn't sleeping, was suicidal. The US government had said, you have to go back to Honduras. And when they were shipped to the airport, the daughter had a complete and total panic attack. And so they brought them back to the prison and put them in, in the tournament camp, put them in isolation, solitary confinement. And what is their story? Their story is this, the mother had a beauty parlor. And when she ran out of money to make the extortion payments, The gang came to her house, put a pistol to her head, a pistol to her daughter's head, and molested her daughter. And molested is the word the mother used, but may well have been raped. gang rape of the daughter. And the daughter feels 14 years old. She's sent back. It's like death. So they're trapped in there. Mother tells me that in three days I was on a Saturday. On Tuesday, it was going to be her 15th birthday at Quinceignetta. And Kincin Yard, it's a big deal in Latino culture. So I said to the prison administrators, hold a Kincin Yard party for this girl, show her that someone cares about her. They said, we can't do that. We can't do anything special for any one child. I said, we'll do it for every 15-year-old child, every 15-year-old girl. No, we can't do that because not everyone's Latino and that'd be discriminating between cultures. And I said, hold a birthday party for every child on their birthday in this facility. Make it a policy. And he said, oh, well, that we can consider. And then they wrote me the next week saying, no, it's too much trouble, can't do it. And it's like, that's a symbol to me, the dehumanizing nature of this. I'm happy to report they're no longer lost. She and her daughter no longer rocked up, but the reason they're not locked up anymore is because Several of us put a lot of attention on it, and the daughter tried to commit suicide and hang herself with a sheet, and then she was released. And now she's in school, and she's all the soccer team, and she's singing in a choir, and she's trying to put her life back together. You know, it's interesting that you mentioned he's in Terra because we just ran a story today that was sort of the flip side of this of a girl who's father was caught up in one of the workplace raids right before her cathedera. And she just felt so guilty about going through with this really important ceremony when her father was locked up. You know, when the Trump administration is raiding places, taking parents away and leaving their kids",
