    "turn_text": "to push to eliminate a healthcare bill that would have resulted in 30 million people losing healthcare. I mean, whatever difference between the Democratic candidates over here Trump is way over here. And in my rural part of my state, the eastern side, it is the congressional district that had more help from the healthcare law than any other district in the country. And it's the most Republican area. And I go out there and people say, thank you for fighting for our hospitals and our clinics. We've doubled the size of our clinic. Now we have people that help with mental health. And now we have people who are helping with drug addiction. And these are some of the best paid jobs in our community. And make sure you preserve the expansion of Medicaid. And so I think there's ways to connect across the country. I came out of high school in 1974 and my parents went through a great leap forward. I'm a blue-collar kid from a little tiny timber town. My folks grew up with my mother particularly so poor that at one point according to family legend her mother was living in a box car and lost her first three kids to the county before she married my grandfather and had four more children. And what they described, their evolution, they couldn't believe that they could on a single mechanics, might have a union mechanic, could buy a house and a car, go camping on vacation, have a small pension, save money for their kids to go to college. And I became the first in the family history to go to college. And I thought, think about what's going to happen in our lifetimes. I mean, our parents went from here to here. We're going to go from here to there. And instead, we've had four decades of my working life in which incomes have been flat or declining for working Americans, while the vast wealth of America has piled up with just a few people. And here's the challenge. The more wealth is piled up among a very few, the more ways that wealth can be deployed to maintain the inequality of income and wealth. and you get stuck there. And I'm worried if we do not make a strong pivot very soon, then the path is paid for the future in which what we've seen for the last four decades will go on into the future. We will not be able to pivot back to an economy that helps lift up all people. You were the first Senator to endorse Bernie Sanders in 2016. I'm wondering if you've decided who you will endorse, or when you will endorse, or where you stand right now on that? Well, just a little correction. I was the first and only. Can Bernie endorse himself? And I was going to say, unless you count Bernie, in which case, full 50th of the Senate endorses",
